Below is a description of what’s going on and a small *SEARCH/REPLACE* patch that will stop “mucking” with any code snippet that already begins with a back‐tick.

1) Bug scenario & root cause  
   • In `createTable(…)`, every `result.code` is blindly wrapped in single back‐ticks:  
     ```js
     case 'code':
       return '`' + value.trim() + '`';
     ```  
   • If the original string already starts with a back‐tick (`` `…``), Markdown sees the nested ticks and then still parses `_underscores_` or `[links](url)` inside the inline‐code as real Markdown, producing `<em>` and `<a>` tags.  
   • The fix is to detect “if it already starts with a back‐tick, leave it alone,” rather than wrap it again.

2) Patch for lighthouse-core/formatters/table.js  
```javascript
### lighthouse-core/formatters/table.js
<<<<<<< SEARCH
          case 'code':
            return '`' + value.trim() + '`';
          case 'pre':
            return '```' + '\n' + result[key].trim() + '```';
=======
          case 'code': {
            const trimmed = value.trim();
            // If the string already starts with a back‐tick, return raw.
            if (trimmed.startsWith('`')) {
              return trimmed;
            }
            // Otherwise wrap in inline‐code ticks.
            return '`' + trimmed + '`';
          }
          case 'pre':
            return '```' + '\n' + result[key].trim() + '```';
>>>>>>> REPLACE
```

With this change, any snippet that already begins with `` ` `` will be passed straight through and not re-wrapped (so no more stray `<em>` or `<a>` tags inside your code).
